t of time in  that data collection and I think you'll  be surprised how little time is actually  spent on optimizing the specific ml  algorithm so as I mentioned before data  qualification is a really important step  as you're thinking about the feasibility  of your ml use cases so this is a list  of steps or a checklist that you can use  as you're thinking about ml use cases  and you're trying to assess their  feasibility as I'm walking through these  think about the data that you have to  support your ml use cases and how it  might measure up so the very first step  is around assessing the feasibility  based on any data ownership or practical  considerations so again machine learning  projects require large amounts of data  and you want to make sure that you have  ownership of that data so that you could  use it to build your ml model in many  cases that's not an issue  so for sales forecasting for example you  probably own a lot of that data yourself  if you're  you know managing the stores where the  sales are being made but sometimes there  are other scenarios where you don't own  the data and that can sometimes be a  blocker so it's important to think about  those practical considerations the next  step is around collecting samples so  once you've identified your ml use case  we want you to consider all of the data  that you currently collect that might be  used in an ml prediction so for the  sales forecasting example you may have  historical sales records from all of  your stores and you'll want to get a  sample of that data as you're assessing  the feasibility of that use case you may  also want to bring in other data sets  though things like promotional data that  have a really strong impact on the  problem as well so identify all those  different data sets and then collect the  samples you can then hand over those  samples to your data analyst who will  take a look at things like data quality  when I say data quality we're looking at  things like are ther